course: dictionary
lesson: 10323
title: Traditions: what we always do
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A tradition is a custom, belief or way of doing things that has been passed down from older people to younger people for a long time. «In the UK and Ireland, tradition says April Fool tricks are done before midday» means people have handed on a habit about when to play pranks.
explain: Traditions can be big, like a festival that a whole country celebrates, or small, like a special thing your family always does on a birthday. Both are traditions because people do them again and again, and they link you to the people who came before you.
explain: A tradition is not always right. «We've always done it this way» is not a reason on its own, because old ways can be good or bad. Respecting someone's tradition does not mean you have to share it, but it does mean you treat it with care.
fact: A tradition is something passed down from older people to younger people.
fact: «Tradition» can mean a custom, a family habit, or the general way things have been done for a long time, as in «by tradition».
fact: «Traditional» is the adjective: a traditional recipe is one that has been made the same way for many years.
fact: Useful phrases: family tradition, keep a tradition alive, break with tradition.
fact: Respecting other people's traditions is good manners, even if you do not share them.
exercise: In the UK and Ireland, what does tradition say about April Fool tricks? | *They are done before midday | They are done only at Christmas | They are done after dinner | They are never done
exercise: What is the best way to show respect for a classmate's tradition? | Ignore it completely | Tell them their tradition is silly | Laugh at it because it is different | *Ask them about it and listen politely
exercise: A family tradition gives people a sense of ___. | rain | boredom | *belonging | hunger
test: «We've always done it this way» is an appeal to tradition. Is that enough reason on its own to keep doing something? | *No, old ways can be good or bad, so check whether they work | Never, because old ways are always wrong | Only if the teacher says so | Yes, always
test: One of the wise moves is to hold your own beliefs with care. Which of these is also a wise move? | Assume you know what others believe | *Discuss ideas and never insult people | Insult people whose ideas are different | Keep quiet about everything
exam: Which is the best description of a tradition? | A rule made only last week | *A custom or way of doing things passed down for a long time | A kind of petrol station | A type of weather
